From 2b35d81dd5a528784c9bcdfab3cfbc42dcf25f87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuk3s <44327449+nuk3s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 11:03:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] server: DeepSeek-V4.1 DSML tool calls stream as they decode

The chat stream, and the /v1/messages and /v1/responses streams made from it, sent a DSML call only at the end of
the reply: commit gave the call streamer only tool_parse.beforeDsml(answer), and openai.stream sent the parsed calls
whole at the finish. Measured on a pair, 2026-10-31, one Write call: through LiteLLM's /v1/messages its 869
characters of arguments came as one input_json_delta after 4.1 s without an event (the engine's own /v1/messages:
885 characters in one delta after 7.2 s). A vLLM server with another model (Qwen3.6) sent its Write call through the
same gateway as 59 deltas over 1.6 s. LiteLLM makes input_json_delta from the OpenAI arguments deltas, and an agent
client's live token counter adds characters/4 for each delta, so the counter stopped during every tool call and
jumped at its end.
- tool_stream.Dsml (Streamer.feedDsml): commit gives it the answer each round, never the reasoning. It makes each
  search that tool_parse.dsml41Calls makes on the finished reply, on the text so far, and a search whose closer has
  not come yet waits. A call's header (index, id, type, name, empty arguments) goes out when its invoke name is whole
  and the parse takes the invoke: an offered tool under its offered spelling, any other tool under the model's name
  (fbbc971 returns such calls). A string value goes out as JSON-escaped pieces while it decodes. A tail that can begin
  its closer, or a character that is not whole, waits. Any other value goes out when its parameter closes, as the
  parse writes it (json.parseText, the raw text as a string when that fails, compact). The escape is per byte (ascii
  off), so the pieces join to the parse's text byte for byte. The streamer stays off for the reply when another call
  form comes before a block.
- A block's first call streams live. Its closing brace waits for the block's closer. When the block makes a second
  call, the first call's brace and every later delta of the block wait in a queue, and the block's closer sends them
  in order. The parse takes no call from a block that never closes, so a block cut short leaves only its first call,
  open: no whole call goes out for a block the parse rejects. The later calls of a block go out at its closer.
- chat.commit ends a block under way (Streamer.feedDsmlBlock) before it sends the round's prose. A text delta between
  a call's header and its last piece ends that call for a client (LiteLLM's /v1/messages stream, /v1/responses), so
  the prose that follows a block in the same round now goes out after the block's last delta. chat.finish does the
  same before the prose tail.
- The streamer turns off for the reply when a text does not extend the text it read (its positions then do not hold
  there), when thinking is off and the answer holds "<|channel|>" (the finish then reads Harmony channels out of the
  text), and when the finish's content does not start with the text it read (Dsml.verify). The finish then follows
  the parse.
- openai.Run.settle: at the finish the sent calls (Dsml.streamed: the calls whose header went out) are checked against
  the parse. When they are the parse's first calls (the same names, the same arguments byte for byte, each closed),
  nothing goes out twice, and the parse's other calls (another call form after the blocks) go out under the next
  indexes. Otherwise the parse dropped or changed what went out: nothing more of the calls goes out, no brace closes a
  call the parse rejects, the content continues to the parse's content, and a warning line is logged. The finish
  reason is the parse's in all cases.
- A reply that ends inside a block (max_tokens, a stop string, the end token, a block without its closer) ends as
  the whole reply reports it: finish_reason length or stop, the content with the block's text, no tool_calls. Its
  open call stays open (an unclosed JSON prefix, as vLLM and the Anthropic API stream a cut call). A repeated
  parameter goes out twice; last-wins JSON readers get the parse's object. A value that holds an invoke's closer
  stops its call's pieces.
- chat.finish gives the whole answer to the streamer once more after a stream sent a DSML call, for what the last
  tokens held back. After DSML calls went out, an XML call that the end token left open goes out once, from settle,
  under the next index (the XML streamer's finish feed sent it under index 0, over the first DSML call).
No switch. Unchanged: requests without tools, whole (non-stream) replies, the one-call policy
(parallel_tool_calls false drops call deltas and sends its call at the finish, as before), XML calls, the V4 spelling.
Tests: dsml_stream_test.zig drives real DeepSeek-V4.1 token sequences (testdata/dsml_stream.json: a trimmed copy of
the tokenizer with the cases' tokens under their real ids) through the chat route and the Messages route: a long
Write, an Edit, two calls, an unoffered tool, DSML in the reasoning and quoted in text, a call cut by max_tokens, a call
form after a block. Byte-token route tests cover the corner cases: a Harmony marker in a value (cut by max_tokens, a
stop string, the end token), prose in the round that closes a block (one and two blocks, six round patterns), a block
cut in its second invoke, and an XML call after a block. The stream reader checks the order: each header once and in
order, the pieces after their header, and no text between a call's header and its last piece. Joined pieces equal
the whole reply's arguments. Streams without tools, without a call, or with the one-call policy match
testdata/dsml_stream_golden.json (recorded without this patch) event for event. tool_stream.zig feeds the parser's
corner cases at six split points. dsml_fuzz_test.zig feeds 14,000 random replies at random split points: well-formed
ones stream the parse's calls byte for byte, and in every one (odd markup, closers inside values, cut anywhere) each
whole streamed call is one of the parse's, in order. Without the code change 10 of the 14 route tests fail (the golden,
quoted-DSML, Harmony-block and prose-order tests pass). zig_test.sh 113/113 and 95/95 (91 and 95 before);
test_lanes.sh 44/44. Only rank 0's tensorfold-native changes (rank 1's tf-dsv41-lanes does not import the server).
